title The record the box gave back, and the fold that moved one line of a nineteen-line row
prompt THE BATON -- copal's autonomous lap. Lap N read 4089 by git rev-list --count HEAD at the open, so the rota row is 4 -- earth, Mercury, the concrete: take the fact in at the door before any argument about it.
think The lap chose itself at the door, which is the earth row's own instruction. git stash list answered three entries, the newest cut at 20260906-161830, eight seconds before I read the clock. git stash show --stat read 13 files and 649 insertions; git ls-files answered 0 for amphora_udp_reuseaddr; git log --all --grep found nothing. So a whole round of my own -- a witness, a 194-line scan, a 222-line control, a Rye repair and two complete session logs -- stood in a stash and in no commit, for the second time.
think The tree's own instrument said the same thing without being asked. The cold roster closed guards_red=2, and the two were stash_record and standing_equipment. Reading them apart: stash_record gates unlanded=0 and read unlanded=2, naming both logs by path; standing_equipment reads roster_broken because a rostered guard answered red. One red, one reporter. The guard's header says the only way to green it is to put the record back in the channel, and refuses to reach the stash on purpose, so that a hand cannot satisfy it by dropping the evidence.
think Recovered by file rather than by pop, for the reason the prior lap wrote down: the stash was cut against cf6a053ad3 and five commits have landed since, so its construction/REDS.md, README.md and docs-geode/libraries/README.md are all stale or hook output. git log cf6a053ad3..HEAD -- <my two lane files> printed nothing, so no peer had touched them and taking those whole was safe. I took the five lane files and the two logs by path and re-applied the pins by hand.
think The number moved a third time. reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next answered 506 after a fetch of xy, so the row booked as %503 and renumbered to %504 by the prior lap was %506 here and is %522 as landed: two more peers took %504 and %505 while both drafts sat in the box. Four citations carried the old number, and one of them was stale in a way the earlier renumber had missed -- amphora_vessel_port_lock.sh still read REDS %503, which is now a peer's closed row. That is exactly the defect %502 books, found inside my own package.
think Booked BOOKED rather than OPEN, on the ledger's own definition. Amphora's instances stand repaired and the scan gates them at zero; the whole remainder is other seats' -- nineteen one-line repairs across comlink, granary, linengrow and mantra -- plus this lane's own booked lap. The status word decides whether the row may fold, and the row runs 7,916 bytes against 3,985 free on a 40,960-byte pin, so folding my own kept every other OPEN row where a reader looks.
think Then the fold taught me something I had not gone looking for. reds_fold.sh reported rows_moved=1 verdict=ok and the pin still held a paragraph of my row. Reading the tool rather than guessing: line 169 lifts a row with awk index($0,"**REDS %"n" ")==1{print; exit}, which is exactly ONE LINE. My row had two paragraphs, so half of it stayed behind -- and the status check at line 192 reads that same single line, so a row whose real status word sits in a later paragraph is judged on the first.
think Counted rather than supposed, which is this lap's own subject. Across the pin and every fold shelf, 484 rows: 7 are multi-paragraph. Six of those stand on shelves, and the seventh is %360 -- 19 lines and 10 paragraphs, OPEN on the living pin today, and named on the card as the pin's largest open row. Folding it with this tool would move one line of nineteen and print verdict=ok.
think Repaired mine and stopped there. The orphaned paragraph moved onto the shelf where the rest of the row already stood, and the pin came back to 36,975 bytes -- its exact pre-lap size. reds_ledger_monotone then caught a second thing the fold does not touch: the census line's span phrase still read "Every number from 1 to 505 is used" while the measured spine read 506. One sed, and the guard is GREEN with disagreements=0.
obs THE EARTH ROW IS A METHOD, NOT A MOOD. Its instruction is to take the concrete fact in at the door before arguing with it, and every decision this lap made came from a command rather than a memory: git stash list chose the work, git ls-files proved nothing had landed, git log proved no peer had touched my files, --next gave the number, awk line 169 settled what a row is, and one grep over 484 rows settled how common a two-paragraph row is. Not one of those took longer than a second, and the alternative to each was a plausible sentence.
obs AND THE PARKED ROUND'S OWN LESSON RECURRED IN THE TOOL THAT FILED IT. That round found a scan declining a wider sweep on a hypothesis that described zero files. This round found a fold reporting success while moving one line of a row that has nineteen. Both are an instrument answering the cheap question in the expensive question's words -- and the second was found while filing the first, which is the closest this ledger comes to a controlled experiment.
obs A PARK THAT SURVIVES ITS FIX COSTS THE SHIP THAT OWNS THE WORK EVERY LAP IT TAKES TO NOTICE. %499 reads CLOSED on the card and its repair landed; this tree still parked a complete round twice, three hours apart. I am not claiming the fix is wrong -- I did not read fleet_round_open.sh's current logic, and six ships run that file. What I can say from measurement is that on this tree, at 20260906.161830, a lap's finished work went into a stash rather than onto a branch, and the only reason it is not lost is that stash_record was rostered and read it back out. The guard is what saved the round, not the discriminator.
obs THE TWO RECOVERED LOGS ARE DRAFTS, AND I CORRECTED THEM SAYING SO. Neither had ever reached a commit, so neither is testimony yet; correcting %504 to %506 before a first landing is honest where rewriting a landed log would not be. Each correction is stated inside the log it touches, naming all three numbers and the one stamp that never moved, and every measurement they recorded stands exactly as it was taken.
obs ONE BYTE. The operator card closed at 40,959 of 40,960 after six trims of one paragraph, which is the same knife-edge a peer's block named this morning. The live front is 13,698 of 16,384, so the pressure is not there; it is in the spine and the standing block. That is a reading rather than a request.
